    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d380">

                <pb facs="0583" n="550" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v23/pg550"/>

                <head>380. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in the Congo<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d380-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1965-01-05T13:25:00-05:00">January 5, 1965,
                            1:25 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1718. Komer of White House staff had long
                    chat with Struelens and discussed among
                    other things Tshombe’s projected visit
                    to U.S. in January.</p>
                <p>The conversation ended with apparent agreement between the two that a U.S. visit
                    at this time would not repeat not be in Tshombe’s interest due continued likelihood Negro demonstrations
                    plus further African charges that he “tool of imperialists” etc. It was
                    therefore agreed Struelens, who leaving
                    here for Leo the 5th, would urge <pb
                        facs="0584" n="551" xml:id="pg551"/>
                    Tshombe postpone visit, concentrate on
                    mending his African fences and organizing elections. If, as expected, he emerges
                    as head of newly elected government, would be welcome here at mutually
                    convenient time.</p>
                <p>Struelens will contact you in Leo and hopes you will see Tshombe with him on this and related matters.
                    Leave this to your discretion but believe you should encourage present
                    constructive approach.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d380-f2"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Rusk</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d380-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d380-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files
                    1964–66, POL 7 THE CONGO. Secret; Immediate. Drafted by McIlvaine, cleared by
                        Komer and Fredericks, and approved by McIlvaine.
                    Repeated to Brussels and London, and to Paris for Harriman.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f2" n="2" xml:id="d380-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 2619 from Leopoldville, January 6, Godley reported that Tshombe, who was “aware of the American Negro leadership’s
                    aversion to him,” had not mentioned to him any visit to the United States since
                    his return from Europe. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
